2	// ---------- gc and weakref ----------
3	// Weak references over the runtime's Rc values, and the cycle collector behind gc.collect(). Brevi's values
4	// are Rc-based, so a reference drops as soon as its last strong owner drops; a cycle never does. CPython's
5	// gc.collect() collects cycles. Brevi starts from the targets of the weakrefs the program made, walks the
6	// value graph those targets can reach, and breaks every cycle that has no strong reference from outside
7	// itself. A cycle nobody weakly referenced is invisible to a program (it has no way to observe the leak), so
8	// starting at weakref targets is enough for every observable cycle.
9	//
10	// The collector is deliberately conservative: any edge it cannot see (inside a closure, an Ext value, a
11	// class's attributes) shows up as an extra strong count, which marks the target reachable and so keeps it.
12	// It never collects a value that is still reachable; it may leak a cycle it could not walk.
